lesson k-my-family v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=3 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "My family"
  goal "Read a family tree of three generations, say who is oldest, and know that every grown-up was once a baby."

  look {
    scene 36x16 {
      familytree tree names=["Grandma Rose", "Grandpa Tom", "Nana Mei", "Grandad Li", "Mum", "Dad", "Sam"] years=[1950, 1948, 1953, 1951, 1982, 1980, 2018] blank=-1 at=canvas(1, 0)
    }
    say """
      This is Sam's family tree. Sam is at the bottom. Above Sam are Mum and Dad, and above them are
      their own parents, Sam's grandparents. The people at the top were born first, a long time ago,
      and they were babies once too.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.once-a-baby k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice k.once-a-baby count=1 seed=2
    practice k.tree-grandparents count=2 seed=3
    practice k.sams-parents count=2 seed=4 easy-count=1
    show k.tree-oldest
    practice k.oldest-person count=1 seed=6
    show k.my-people
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.grandma-a-child
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.three-in-order
  }
  remember {
    say "Grandparents, then parents, then children. Everyone was a baby once."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud. Families come in many shapes, and Sam's tree is only one of them:
    when the child talks about their own family, any grown-ups who look after them count, and there
    is no right number of anybody. Photographs of the child's grown-ups as children are the best way
    into the idea that old people were once young. If the child is stuck, draw your own family as
    boxes and lines together. England, Reception, ELG Past and Present (recalled, not fetched):
    "Talk about the lives of the people around them and their roles in society".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a parent as a baby is worked in the teacher's pen first, the counts are the grandparents and the parents, and Sam's parents are found."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the counts are everybody and the rows, and the questions follow a line up to Mum's own parents and find the youngest."
  }
}
